Q: Error &#39;Cannot apply $pull to a non-array value&#39; A: <p>Your usage of <a href="https://docs.mongodb.com/manual/reference/operator/update/pull/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>$pull</code></a> is incorrect:</p>

<pre><code>db.collection(USERDETAILS_COLLECTION).findOneAndUpdate(
  { "emailVerificationHistory.verifyCode":mailCode },
  {'$pull':{ "emailVerificationHistory": { "verifyCode": mailCode } },
  function(err,pullData) {
      if(err) {
          console.log(err);
      } else {
          res.send({msg: 'User Verified Succesfully', status: 1});
      }
  }
);
</code></pre>

<p>The <a href="https://docs.mongodb.com/manual/reference/operator/update/pull/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>$pull</code></a> update operator works like a query in itself. So it takes a condition and "pulls" the matching elements from the array.</p>

<p>So whilst it is good practice to use a selection query to match the element in the array, you don't use the positional <code>$</code> operator to the element, but rather supply the array itself.</p>

Q: HTML5 nested lists: Indented list item without parent possible? A: <p>I don't normally log into SO while I'm at work, but this question and the lack of suitable answers just has me unable to resist.  The other answer (use css on the list item) is the best path forward for majority of use cases, including the asker, but it does not answer the actual question that is being asked, which is producing the particular visual result with Valid HTML</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I conclude that there really is no way to produce this formatting with valid HTML5</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is incorrect, but the answer is ugly, frustrating, and verbose.</p>
<p>The semantically-correct answer is that a nested list item is inside a nested list inside a parent list item.  The tree looks like <code>ul &gt; li &gt; ul &gt; li</code>  It's not a pretty answer, and I myself am going to go with the css approach for my use case.  But this is the... technically correct answer.</p>
<p>Basically, if you don't do this extra nesting step, screenreaders will not read the nested list items as nested, but instead as normal top-level nested items, which is a problem for accessibility.  But you absolutely CAN achieve this visual appearance while having correctly-nested lists (correct according to HTML5 validators, screenreader technology, Lighthouse, etc).</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;ul&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Top Level Item 1&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;
    Top Level Item 2
    &lt;ul&gt;
      &lt;li&gt;Second Level Item 1 (Normal way.  This sub-list is tied to item 2.  Intended way to use HTML lists.)&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;li&gt;Second Level Item 2&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;li&gt;Second Level Item 3&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Top Level Item 3&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;
    &amp;nbsp;
    &lt;ul&gt;
      &lt;li&gt;Second Level Item 1 (Empty Parent.  Achieved with `&amp;amp;nbsp;` before sub-list to reserve the space where the parent would be.).  Screenreaders will see this as items on a sub-list&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;li&gt;Second Level Item 2&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;li&gt;Second Level Item 3&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Top Level Item 5&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li style="list-style:none"&gt;
    &lt;ul&gt;
      &lt;li&gt;List Item seemingly without parent (under label-less top level item 6, achieved with `list-style: none` on parent `li`).  Screenreaders will see this as an item on a sub-list &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Top Level Item 7&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li style="list-style:circle; margin-left: 40px;"&gt;Top Level Item 8 - For comparison.  styled as nested, not actually nested.  Screenreaders will think this is at the same level as the other items.  May be easier to achieve on-the-fly with tab-indenting from the user input.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
